  urldate = {2026-02-27},
  abstract = {A technique proposed for increasing the speech intelligibility of persons with profound sensorineural hearing loss is described. The processing is based on a modification of the chirp-z transform that allows for nonlinear sampling along an arbitrary contour of the z plane. Although the theory allows for the nonlinear sampling to be arbitrary, an example that is appropriate for nonlinear spectrum compression to aid the hearing impaired is given: samples are linearly spaced along the unit circle for a specified low-frequency segment; beyond the break-point the samples are nonlinearly spaced, perhaps spiraling inward to enhance spectral peaks, with the spacing width increasing with increasing frequency following an exponential function. A standard inverse DFT of these frequency samples returns the spectrum-compressed sequence. Processing of continuous speech requires windowing and processing the data in a manner similar to the short-time Fourier transform (STFI’). The paper presents the theoretical basis for, and development of, the algorithm.},
